# DESCRIPTION

There are three streams, `stdin`, `stdout` and `stderr`, with file descriptors 0, 1 and 2, and no others: a `FILE` is one of these three.
Nothing in the library makes a stream over a file.

## Output

The library keeps no buffer for output: writes to `stdout` and `stderr` go straight to the runtime, which gathers `stdout` by line.
A process started with standard output and error rings writes to them.
Any other writes `stdout` as the process's debuglog, one record for each line; a line of more than 216 bytes is split into records of that size, and a partial line stays in the library until its newline or until `fflush`, a write to `stderr`, a read of `stdin`, or `exit`.
`stderr` is its own ring when the process has one for descriptor 2, and `stdout`'s output flushed at once otherwise.
Writing to `stdin` fails with `EBADF`.

`printf`, `fprintf`, `vprintf` and `vfprintf` write the formatted text to a stream (`printf` to `stdout`) and return the number of bytes written.
`snprintf` and `vsnprintf` write at most <n> bytes including the terminating NUL, and return the length the whole output has, as C99 does; with <n> of 0 they write nothing and <s> may be null.
`sprintf` and `vsprintf` are `snprintf` and `vsnprintf` without a limit.
The `v` forms take a `va_list` that they copy, so the caller's is left as it was.

## Formats

A conversion is `%`, then flags, a width, a precision and a length, then the conversion character.
The flags are `-`, `+`, space, `#` and `0`.
The width and the precision are numbers or `*`; a negative `*` width is `-` with its magnitude, and a negative `*` precision is none.
The lengths are `hh`, `h`, `l`, `ll`, `j`, `z`, `t` and, for floating point, `L`.
The conversions are:

: `d i`
  A signed decimal integer.
: `u o x X`
  An unsigned decimal, octal or hexadecimal integer.
: `f F e E g G a A`
  A `double`, or with `L` a `long double`: x87's 80-bit format on amd64, binary128 on arm64 and rv64.
  The digits are exact: the result is the correctly rounded decimal expansion of the value, whatever its size.
: `c`
  A byte.
: `s`
  A string; a null pointer prints `(null)`, as glibc does.
: `p`
  A pointer, as `%#lx`; a null pointer prints `(nil)`.
: `%`
  A percent sign.

An unknown conversion ends the formatting and the function returns -1; the text before it has been written.
`%n`, `%b`, the wide-character conversions `%lc` and `%ls` (read as `%c` and `%s`), `%m` and positional arguments (`%1$d`) are not supported.

## Input

Only `stdin` can be read.
Reading another stream fails and sets its error indicator and `errno` to `EBADF`.
`stdin` is read through a buffer of 4096 bytes from the process's standard input ring, or is empty if the process has none; standard output is flushed before each read.

`fgetc`, `getc` and `getchar` return the next byte as an `unsigned char`, or `EOF` at the end of input.
`fgets` reads up to <n> - 1 bytes, stopping after a newline, terminates them with a NUL and returns <s>; it returns a null pointer if it read nothing.
`fread` reads up to <n> items of <size> bytes and returns the number of whole items read.

`feof` and `ferror` return the end-of-file and error indicators of a stream; `clearerr` clears both.
The end-of-file indicator is not sticky: the next read asks the ring again.

## Other calls

`fputc`, `putc` and `putchar` write one byte and return it as an `unsigned char`, or `EOF` on error.
`fputs` and `puts` write a string (`puts` adds a newline, and neither writes the terminating NUL) and return 0, or `EOF` on error.
`fwrite` writes <size> times <n> bytes and returns <n>.
`fflush` flushes `stdout`'s partial line whatever its argument, which may be null, and returns 0.
`fileno` returns the descriptor, 0, 1 or 2.
`flockfile` and `funlockfile` do nothing, and `ftrylockfile` returns 0 (the lock is taken): the streams are written whole.

`remove` removes a file or an empty directory, and `rename` renames a file; they are the calls of unistd(2) and stat(2) under C's names, and fail the same way.
A program built without the file library finds both failing with `ENOENT`.

