# DIAGNOSTICS

`fopen` always returns a null pointer with `errno` set to `ENOENT`, whatever the file and mode.
`fclose` closes nothing and returns 0.

# BUGS

`fprintf` and `vfprintf` on `stderr` write through `stdout`'s path, not `stderr`'s: the text appears with standard output, buffered to the end of the line, and does not go to the stderr ring.
`fputs`, `fwrite` and `fputc` on `stderr` do go to it.
A program that needs its diagnostics on standard error uses `fwrite` or `write` of unistd(2).

There are no file streams, so no `setvbuf`, `fseek`, `ftell`, `ungetc`, `scanf`, `tmpfile`, `perror` or `getline`.
Mixing `getchar` with `read(0, ...)` loses data: the stream keeps what it read ahead.
`fread` and `fgets` cannot tell an error from the end of input.
